{
  "teamId": 139,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Tampa Bay Rays entered the season with cautious optimism, riding a power-laden roster anchored by Evan Longoria, Logan Morrison, and a bullpen built around closer Alex Colomé. April was immediately turbulent — a rough 12-14 record masked some genuine bright spots, including timely hits from Morrison and a walk-off feel from Jesús Sucre's clutch single late in the month. The bullpen showed early cracks, with Xavier Cedeño and Jumbo Diaz serving up damaging home runs, but the offense showed enough pop to keep things interesting.\n\nMay turned into the Rays' best month, a 17-13 stretch fueled by the long ball and a sense that this team might actually contend. Tim Beckham's three-run blast, Kevin Kiermaier's late solo shot, and Corey Dickerson's clutch single against Minnesota gave the offense a legitimate identity. The rotation and pen wobbled — Danny Farquhar surrendered a three-run bomb from Elvis Andrus — but the bats bailed the team out often enough to post a winning record. For a brief moment in early June, the Rays looked like a dark horse wild card threat.\n\nJune and July, however, told a grimmer story. The team went a combined 25-26 over those two months, and the cracks in the roster widened into fault lines. Alex Colomé, usually the team's most dependable weapon, blew critical late-inning leads twice in four days in late June, allowing game-changing hits to Pittsburgh and Baltimore. Chris Archer was torched in the heart of the order, and a devastating Tim Beckham double play killed a key July rally against the Angels. The bullpen's unreliability became a defining narrative, and the team could never string together enough consecutive wins to mount a real playoff push.\n\nAugust and September offered flashes of what might have been. Logan Morrison crushed his 31st home run, Kevin Kiermaier delivered a stunning clutch blast in mid-September, and Alex Colomé's defense bailed him out with a crucial double play against Minnesota. But at 80-82, the Rays finished below .500 for the third straight season — a team with genuine power (228 home runs) and a solid ERA (3.97) that simply could not put it all together. The 2017 Rays were a study in almost: talented enough to tease, inconsistent enough to disappoint.",
